@verbs mail letters mailbox
@tier player
@title mail: how to read and send letters
@see nicknames

  mail
  mail <names>

At a post office, 'mail' opens your mailbox and lists your letters; the
ones you have not read yet carry an N. With names, you write a letter to
those characters without going through the mailbox.

Inside the mailbox:

  <number>                  read that letter
  l                         list your letters
  m <names>                 write a letter
  r <number>                reply to whoever sent it
  f <number> <names>        forward a letter
  d <numbers>               mark letters to be deleted when you quit
  u <numbers>               unmark them
  g                         show your groups
  g <group> <names>         add names to a group
  g <group> -<name>         remove a name from a group
  q                         quit, deleting the marked letters
  ?                         show the commands

Numbers can go alone or in ranges: 3, 1-4 or 2,5.

Writing a letter asks for the subject and then the text, line by line. A
line with just a dot ends it and '~q' discards it. Afterwards you can
send a copy to other characters.

A group is your own name for several characters: writing to it writes to
all of them. Nicknames work as recipients too.

A letter that arrives while you are connected tells you so; otherwise you
will know when you log in. Letters older than two months are lost. Guests
have no mailbox and cannot receive letters.
